  "worked_solution": "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5y & =0 \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta -5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  & =5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ so our first solution is ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are $\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$",
